From 39f22f65b21655a97ef1fe89dcc8230e3526f480 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 11:12:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Far spaces: offsets, provenance, built-ins, warning 2026,
 bytecode error

- farxoN: the address in A and X plus an offset under 256 in Y,
  translated, for accesses at an offset (13 bytes a call instead of an
  inline add before farxlN).
- A pointer value keeps the space it points into (the address of far
  data, pointer arithmetic), so (struct ext *) &r->x[0] lands in the
  space; casts and conversions that take a far pointer outside its
  space give warning 2026; casts keep the space of a pointer still in
  memory, and so does a pre-increment's result.
- The built-in strcpy, memcpy and memset translate their far operands;
  a block that is not a whole far object of the pointer's type warns.
- Far data in byte code is an error.
- The linker leaves far data out of its cross-bank check, and writes
  the upper halves of banks past 63 that hold data there.
- autotest/farspacetest.c (native only).

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
